<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christoph Willibald Gluck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1714">1714</a>–<a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christoph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibald">Willibald</a> Gluck was a German-born composer who worked for much of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> court. With the librettist Ranieri de' Calzabigi he wrote Orfeo ed Euridice of 1762 and Alceste of 1767, reforming opera seria by subordinating virtuoso display to drama and simple, expressive music. His later operas for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, such as Iphigénie en Tauride, influenced Mozart and the course of nineteenth-century opera.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christoph-willibald-gluck.jpg" alt="Christoph Willibald Gluck" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph-Siffred Duplessis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Siffred_Duplessis_-_Christoph_Willibald_Gluck_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gluck was born in the Upper Palatinate, probably on 2 July 1714, the date he himself gave on a document requested from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1785, and was baptised on 4 July. His father Alexander was a forester. In 1727 the family moved to Eisenberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, where Alexander became head forester to Prince Philipp Hyazinth von Lobkowitz. About 1727 or 1728 Gluck went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, left without a degree, and probably reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1734. In 1737 he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and studied with Giovanni Battista Sammartini. His first opera, Artaserse, was performed on 26 December 1741, and he went on to write for Milan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, mostly to texts by Metastasio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and with touring companies, Gluck married Maria Anna Bergin on 15 September 1750 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a>. Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv">Benedict XIV</a> made him a Knight of the Golden Spur in 1756. With the choreographer Gasparo Angiolini he wrote the ballet Don Juan (1761). Orfeo ed Euridice, first performed on 5 October 1762 to a libretto by Ranieri de' Calzabigi, subordinated star singers to drama and did away with dry recitative. The preface to Alceste (1767) set out the principles: no da capo arias, no virtuoso display, simpler melodic lines and an overture linked to the action.</p><p>Under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> he wrote eight operas for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Iphigénie en Aulide, premiered on 19 April 1774, set off a quarrel between Gluckists and supporters of the Italian composer Niccolò Piccinni. Iphigénie en Tauride (1779) was a great success, but Echo et Narcisse failed after twelve performances, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gluck died on 15 November 1787, a few hours after a heart arrhythmia struck while he lunched with friends, at the age of 73. Only half of his work survived a fire in 1809. It includes about 35 full-length operas and numerous ballets and instrumental works. His reforms influenced Mozart, particularly Idomeneo (1781), and left a school of disciples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, among them Antonio Salieri, Cherubini, Méhul and Spontini. Berlioz was his most devoted French admirer, and his example influenced Weber and Wagner.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christoph Willibald Gluck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…architecture of Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach and Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt, the Karlskirche, the Belvedere and the abbey of Melk defined a Central European style, and in the later eighteenth century <b>Christoph Willibald Gluck</b>, Joseph Haydn and Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart created the Viennese Classical style in music.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Christoph Willibald Gluck</b>'s Orfeo ed Euridice was first performed at the Burgtheater in Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-5">5 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Erasbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.15,11.42&amp;year=1761" aria-label="Erasbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4813 449 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christoph Willibald Gluck"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.15,11.42&amp;year=1761">What was here in 1761? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
